  "id": "DrugBank_DB00438 - MONDO_20920",
  "verbalization": "Ceftazidime, a broad-spectrum antibiotic, is implicated in the treatment of Escherichia coli infections through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Ceftazidime is associated with the treatment of endometritis and rhinoscleroma, both of which are linked to Escherichia coli infections. The shared indication of Aztreonam, a related antibiotic, further supports a mechanistic link between Ceftazidime and Escherichia coli infection, as Aztreonam is also used to treat similar infections. The presence of Cefoxitin, another antibiotic with a similar mode of action, in the pathway for endometritis reinforces the idea that Ceftazidime may act through a common mechanism in the treatment of bacterial infections, particularly those caused by Escherichia coli. The integrated evidence suggests that Ceftazidime may exert its antibacterial effect through a mechanism that is consistent with the inhibition of bacterial growth, possibly by targeting the bacterial cell wall or other essential cellular processes. The observed scores indicate a strong, though not absolute, correlation between Ceftazidime and Escherichia coli infections, supported by the shared indications and the consistent mode of action across different antibiotics.",
